1	001	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: gathering	Ordinal	Dependence on the gathering of wild plants and small land fauna, relative to other subsistence activities.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
2	002	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: hunting	Ordinal	Dependence on hunting, including trapping and fowling, relative to other subsistence activities.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
3	003	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: fishing	Ordinal	Dependence on fishing, including shellfishing and the pursuit of large aquatic animals, relative to other subsistence activities.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
4	004	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: animal husbandry	Ordinal	Dependence on animal husbandry, relative to other subsistence activities.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
5	005	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: agriculture	Ordinal	Dependence on agriculture, relative to other subsistence activities.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
6	006	Marriage, Kinship, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Transactions at marriage: prevailing type	Categorical	Prevailing type of transfer or exchange at marriage.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
7	007	Marriage, Kinship, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Transactions at marriage: alternate type	Categorical	Alternative types of transfer or exchange at marriage, or supplementary practices.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
8	008	Marriage, Kinship	Domestic organization	Categorical	The prevailing form of domestic or familial organization.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
9	009	Marriage, Kinship	Marital composition: monogamy and polygamy	Categorical	Marital composition of family units, regardless of whether families live as independent nuclear units or as part of larger extended families.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
10	010	Marriage, Kinship	Marital residence with kin: first years	Categorical	Pattern of marital residence in the first years or so of marriage. The variable "Marital residence with kin: change after first years" indicates whether or not this differs from the pattern of marital residence in later years.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
11	011	Marriage, Kinship	Transfer of residence at marriage: prevailing pattern	Categorical	The prevailing pattern of transfer of residence at marriage (note: not in original EA, summarized from variable describing marital residence with kin).		Gray (1999)
12	012	Marriage, Kinship	Marital residence with kin: prevailing pattern	Categorical	The prevailing profile of marital residence in the society.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
13	013	Marriage, Kinship	Transfer of residence at marriage: alternate	Categorical	Culturally patterned alternatives to, or numerically significant deviations from, the prevailing pattern of transfer of residence at marriage (note: not in original EA, summarized from variable describing marital residence with kin).		Gray (1999)
14	014	Marriage, Kinship	Marital residence with kin: alternate	Categorical	Culturally patterned alternatives to, or numerically significant deviations from, the prevailing profile of marital residence.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
15	015	Marriage, Kinship, Community organization	Community marriage organization	Categorical	The prevalence of local endogamy, agamy, and exogamy, together with the presence or absence of localized kin groups. The specific structure of clans, whether ambilocal, matrilocal, or patrilocal, is revealed by the variable describing prevailing patterns of residence with kin.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
16	016	Marriage, Kinship, Community organization	Organization of clan communities	Categorical	Indicates whether or not societies organized as clan-communities (i.e., where communities represent a single, localized, exogamous kin group or clan) are also segmented into clan barrios.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
17	017	Kinship	Largest patrilineal kin group	Categorical	The largest type of patrilineal kin group reported for the society.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
18	018	Kinship	Largest patrilineal exogamous group	Categorical	The largest patrilineal kin group characterized by exogamy.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
19	019	Kinship	Largest matrilineal kin group	Categorical	The largest type of matrilineal kin group reported for the society.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
20	020	Kinship	Largest matrilineal exogamous group	Categorical	The largest matrilineal kin group characterized by exogamy.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
21	021	Kinship	Cognatic kin groups	Categorical	The presence or probable absence, and the typology, of cognatic kin groups.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
22	022	Kinship	Secondary cognatic kin group: kindreds and ramages	Categorical	The presence or probable absence, and the typology, of secondary cognatic kin groups. This variable highlights societies for which both kindreds (bilateral descent) and ramages (ambilineal descent) are reported.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
23	023	Marriage, Kinship	Cousin marriages permitted	Categorical	The rules or practices governing the marriageability or nonmarriageability of a man's first cousins and, in some cases, second cousins.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
24	024	Marriage, Kinship	Cousin marriages permitted: subtypes	Categorical	Number of types of cousins (first and/or second cousins) a man is permitted to marry.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
25	025	Marriage, Kinship	Cousin marriages preferred	Categorical	Preferred rather than just permitted marriages to particular first (and/or second) cousins.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
26	026	Marriage, Kinship	Cousin marriages preferred: subtypes	Categorical	Specific type(s) of cousin preferred as spouses.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
27	027	Kinship	Kin terms for cousins	Categorical	Extent to which names for cousins distinguish cousin subtypes and/or among other groups of kin.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
28	028	Subsistence, Economy	Agriculture: intensity	Categorical	Intensity of cultivation.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
29	029	Subsistence, Ecology	Agriculture: major crop type	Categorical	Principal type of crop cultivated.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
30	030	Community organization, Housing, Settlement	Settlement patterns	Categorical	The prevailing type of settlement pattern.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
31	031	Community organization, Population, Settlement	Mean size of local communities	Ordinal	The average population of local communities, whatever the pattern of settlement, computed from census data or other evidence.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
32	032	Community organization	Jurisdictional hierarchy of local community	Ordinal	The number of jurisdictional levels in the local community, with 2 representing the theoretical minimum (e.g., independent nuclear or polygynous families) and 4 representing the theoretical maximum (e.g., nuclear families nested within extended families and clan-barrios). See the variable "Jurisdictional hierarchy beyond the local community" for information on organization beyond the local community. Note that different types of organization on the same level, e.g., a consanguineal lineage and its localized equivalent, are counted as one, and organizations not held to be legitimate, e.g., imposed colonial regimes, are excluded.		Gray (1999)
33	033	Community organization, Politics	Jurisdictional hierarchy beyond local community	Ordinal	The number of jurisdictional levels beyond the local community, with 1 representing the theoretical minimum (e.g.,  none/autonomous bands or villages) and 4 representing the theoretical maximum (e.g., villages nested within parishes, districts, provinces, and a complex state). This variable also provides a measure of political complexity, ranging from 1 for stateless societies, through 2 or 3 for petty and larger paramount chiefdoms or their equivalent, to 4 or 5 for large states. Note that different types of organization on the same level are counted as one, and organizations not held to be legitimate, e.g., imposed colonial regimes, are excluded.		Gray (1999)
34	034	Ritual	Religion: high gods	Categorical	The range of beliefs in high gods. A high god is defined, following Swanson, as a spiritual being who is believed to have created all reality and/or to be its ultimate governor, even if his sole act was to create other spirits who, in turn, created or control the natural world.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
35	035	Games	Games	Categorical	Types of games practiced. Following Roberts, only games with an outcome, i.e., a winner and a loser, are treated.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
36	036	Ritual, Gender, Marriage	Post-partum sex taboos	Ordinal	The normal duration of post-partum sex taboos, i.e., those requiring a lactating mother to refrain from sexual intercourse.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
37	037	Ritual, Gender	Male genital mutilations	Categorical	The practice of circumcision or any of its variants, when culturally normative and not sporadic or merely optional, as well as the age at which it typically occurs.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
38	038	Ritual, Gender, Marriage	Segregation of adolescent boys	Categorical	Degree and mode of segregating boys at or approaching puberty.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
39	039	Subsistence, Economy	Domestic animals: plow cultivation	Categorical	Indicates whether or not animals are employed in plow cultivation, and whether plow cultivation is aboriginal or dates to the post-contact period.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
40	040	Subsistence, Ecology	Domestic animals: type	Categorical	The predominant type of animals kept.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
41	041	Subsistence, Economy	Domestic animals: milking	Categorical	Indicates whether or not domestic animals milked.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
42	042	Subsistence, Economy	Subsistence economy: dominant activity	Categorical	Dominant mode of subsistence (note: not in original EA; derived from other variables on subsistence and type of agriculture).		Gray (1999)
43	043	Kinship	Descent: major type	Categorical	Major mode of descent (note: not in original EA; derived from variables describing patrilineal and matrilineal kin groups EA017, EA019 and EA021).		Gray (1999)
44	044	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: metal working	Categorical	Specialization by sex in such arts as smelting, casting, and forging, i.e., only those which involve the application of fire.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
45	045	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: weaving	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the manufacture of true cloth on a loom or frame; not including the manufacture of nets, baskets, mats, or nonwoven fabrics like barkcloth or felt.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
46	046	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: leather working	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the dressing of skins, e.g., by tanning; not including the manufacture of artifacts from raw hides or undressed skins.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
47	047	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: pottery making	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the manufacture of earthenware utensils.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
48	048	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: boat building	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the construction of true water craft; not including the making of simple floats, rafts, or the like.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
49	049	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: house construction	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the actual building of a permanent dwelling or the erection of a transportable shelter; not including the acquisition or preliminary preparation of the materials used.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
50	050	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: gathering	Categorical	Specialization by sex in the gathering of wild plants and small land fauna.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
51	051	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: hunting	Categorical	Specialization by sex in hunting, including trapping and fowling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
52	052	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex Differences: fishing	Categorical	Specialization by sex in fishing, including shellfishing and the pursuit of large aquatic animals.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
53	053	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: animal husbandry	Categorical	Specialization by sex in animal husbandry.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
54	054	Labour, Gender, Economy	Sex differences: agriculture	Categorical	Specialization by sex in agriculture.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
55	055	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: metal working	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in such arts as smelting, casting, and forging, i.e., only those which involve the application of fire.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
56	056	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: weaving	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the manufacture of true cloth on a loom or frame; not including the manufacture of nets, baskets, mats, or nonwoven fabrics like barkcloth or felt.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
57	057	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: leather working	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the dressing of skins, e.g., by tanning; not including the manufacture of artifacts from raw hides or undressed skins.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
58	058	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: pottery making	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the manufacture of earthenware utensils.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
59	059	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: boat building	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the construction of true water craft; not including the making of simple floats, rafts, or the like.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
60	060	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: house construction	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the actual building of a permanent dwelling or the erection of a transportable shelter; not including the acquisition or preliminary preparation of the materials used.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
61	061	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: gathering	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in the gathering of wild plants and small land fauna.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
62	062	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: hunting	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in hunting, including trapping and fowling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
63	063	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: fishing	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in fishing, including shellfishing and the pursuit of large aquatic animals.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
64	064	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: animal husbandry	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in animal husbandry.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
65	065	Labour, Economy	Age or occupational specialization: agriculture	Categorical	Age or occupational specialization in agriculture.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
66	066	Politics, Class, Economy	Class differentiation: primary	Categorical	The degree and type of class differentiation, excluding purely political and religious statuses. See also "Class differentiation: secondary," as some societies exhibit important features of two different types of class differentation.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
67	067	Politics, Class, Economy	Class differentiation: secondary features	Categorical	Secondary type of class differentiation present in the society, excluding purely political and religious statuses. See also "Class differentiation: primary".		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
68	068	Politics, Class, Economy	Caste differentiation: primary	Categorical	The degree and type of caste differentiation. See also "Caste differentiation: secondary," as some societies exhibit important features of two different types of caste differentation.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
69	069	Politics, Class, Economy	Caste differentiation: secondary features	Categorical	Secondary type of caste differentiation. See also "Caste differentiation: primary".		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
70	070	Politics, Class, Economy, Labour	Slavery: type	Categorical	The forms and prevalence of slave status, treated quite independently of both class and caste status.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
71	071	Politics, Class, Economy, Labour	Slavery: timing	Categorical	Indicates whether slavery present at the time of observation of the society, or only in the past.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
72	072	Politics, Leadership	Political succession	Categorical	Rules of succession applying to the office of local headman (or a close equivalent), without reference to rules of succession prevailing on higher levels of political integration.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
73	073	Politics, Leadership, Kinship	Political succession: hereditary succession	Categorical	Rules of succession applying to the office of local headman (or a close equivalent), without reference to rules of succession prevailing on higher levels of political integration.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
74	074	Property, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Inheritance rule for real property (land)	Categorical	The rule or practice governing the disposition or transmission of a man's property in land (exclusive of any dower right of his widow). [Note that Murdock (1963a) warned that the codes for this variable had "proved inadequate and in serious need of revision," and that the coded data "should consequently be used only with circumspection"].		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
75	075	Property, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Inheritance distribution for real property (land)	Categorical	Following the inheritance rule for real property (land), this variable indicates how real property is distributed among several individuals of the same category. [Note that Murdock (1963a) warned that the codes for this variable had "proved inadequate and in serious need of revision," and that the coded data "should consequently be used only with circumspection"].		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
76	076	Property, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Inheritance rule for movable property	Categorical	The rule or practice governing the disposition or transmission of a man's movable property (exclusive of any dower right of his widow). [Note that Murdock (1963a) warned that the codes for this variable had "proved inadequate and in serious need of revision," and that the coded data "should consequently be used only with circumspection"].		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
77	077	Property, Wealth Transactions, Economy	Inheritance distribution for movable property	Categorical	Following the inheritance rule for movable property, this variable indicates how movable property is distributed among several individuals of the same category. [Note that Murdock (1963a) warned that the codes for this variable had "proved inadequate and in serious need of revision," and that the coded data "should consequently be used only with circumspection"].		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
78	078	Ritual, Gender, Marriage	Norms of premarital sexual behavior of girls	Categorical	Prevailing standards of sex behavior for unmarried women.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
79	079	Community organization, Housing	House construction: ground plan	Categorical	Ground plan of the prevailing type of dwelling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
80	080	Community organization, Housing	House construction: floor level	Categorical	Floor level of the prevailing type of dwelling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
81	081	Community organization, Housing, Ecology	House construction: wall material	Categorical	Wall material of the prevailing type of dwelling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
82	082	Community organization, Housing	House construction: shape of roof	Categorical	Shape of roof  of the prevailing type of dwelling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
83	083	Community organization, Housing, Ecology	House construction: roofing materials	Categorical	Roofing materials of the prevailing type of dwelling.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
84	084	Community organization, Housing	House construction (secondary house type): ground plan	Categorical	Ground plan of secondary or alternative house type.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
85	085	Community organization, Housing	House construction (secondary house type): floor level	Categorical	Floor level of secondary or alternative house type.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
86	086	Community organization, Housing, Ecology	House construction (secondary house type): wall material	Categorical	Wall material of secondary or alternative house type.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
87	087	Community organization, Housing	House construction (secondary house type): shape of roof	Categorical	Shape of roof of secondary or alternative house type.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
88	088	Community organization, Housing, Ecology	House construction (secondary house type): roofing materials	Categorical	Roofing materials of secondary or alternative house type.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
89	090	Politics, Leadership, Economy	Political integration 	Categorical	Political integration of the society with neighbouring communities and/or a larger state; this is the version that appeared in Murdock (1957)'s World Ethnographic Sample (WES).		Murdock (1957); Gray (1999)
90	094	Politics, Leadership	Political succession: version from Murdock (1957)	Categorical	Rule of succession; for cross-cultural comparibility this is the prevailing rule of succession for the headman of a local community or a near equivalent thereto; this is the version that appeared in Murdock (1957)'s World Ethnographic Sample (WES).		Murdock (1957); Gray (1999)
91	112	Ritual	Trance states	Categorical	Presence/absence of culturally patterned forms of altered states of consciousness. Codes distinguish between two types of altered states: PossessionTrance, considered to be a manifestation of spirit possession, and a Trance state that is not explained by possession.		Bourguignon (1973); Gray (1999)
92	113	Ritual, Politics	Societal rigidity	Categorical	Degree of rigidity in social structures. Considers stratification of society, whether status distinctions are earned or ascribed, degree of hierarchy of political system, extent to which residence and group membership are fixed, centralization of authority, flexibility of religious rites.		Greenbaum (1973); Gray (1999)
93	201	Marriage, Kinship	Marital residence with kin: change after first years	Categorical	Indicates whether or not the pattern of marital residence differs in the first years of marriage relative to later years.		Murdock (1962-1971); Gray (1999)
94	202	Population	Population size	Continuous	Population of ethnic group as a whole, unless otherwise noted in "Comments". Note that source differs by society; EA bibliography is source where possible, otherwise Ember (1992).		Murdock (1962-1971); Ember (1992)
